# carts/shmup — scripted walkthrough, re-planned for 160x90 (M4 stage 4.0, Р20/Р25).
# Hand-written (unlike carts/snake's golden.input, which is generated by a planner): every
# enemy in this cartridge is stationary, so hitting one is a question of parking the ship
# under its column and firing, not of tracking motion.
#
# Grammar (quarp replay record --input-file, InputScript.cs): tick:buttons, entries
# separated by commas or newlines, '#' starts a comment. Buttons are held from one entry
# until the next, so every tap (movement release, single shot) needs an explicit follow-up
# entry with an empty button list. An entry at tick N is seen by the cartridge on its own
# tick N+1 (Ticks is incremented before Update); every tick quoted below is cartridge-scale.
#
# What the move to 160x90 changed, and what it did not. The wave clock is pure time
# (90/290/380/580/670/870) and did not move a tick. The geometry did: the four-wide
# formations now sit at x = 55/69/83/97 (were 39/53/67/81), the three-wide one at 62/76/90,
# the ship starts at x=76 (were 60) and flies at y=80 (was 62). That last one is what forced
# the schedule below to be rewritten rather than shifted: a player bullet now needs 28 ticks
# to reach row 0 instead of 19, so the old "drift, then shoot" pacing killed wave 0's last
# enemy at tick 125 — six ticks after it would have fired back. The four shots are therefore
# fired the instant the ship arrives under each column, with no idle ticks in between.
#
#   ticks 2-22    hold Left: ship drifts from its start x=76 to x=55 — wave 0's column 0.
#   tick 23       tap O: one shot from x=55. It clears the AABB gate at spawn+28 == tick 51
#                 (row 0, y=16); column 0's enemy is not scheduled to fire until tick 110.
#   ticks 24-37   hold Right: x 55 -> 69, column 1.
#   tick 38       tap O; column-1 enemy dies at tick 66 (its own fire tick is 113).
#   ticks 39-52   hold Right: x 69 -> 83, column 2.
#   tick 53       tap O; column-2 enemy dies at tick 81 (its own fire tick is 116).
#   ticks 54-67   hold Right: x 83 -> 97, column 3 — the last of wave 0's four enemies.
#   tick 68       tap O; column-3 enemy dies at tick 96, 23 ticks before its own scheduled
#                 fire at 119. Wave 0 (4 enemies, all of them) is fully shot down and not one
#                 of its four ever got a shot off — the ship takes zero damage.
#
#   From tick 96 the field is empty and the ship is still: the frame hash does not change
#   again until tick 290, when wave 1 spawns. That static stretch is the observable proof
#   that the wave was cleared, and it is why nothing is fired after tick 68.
#
#   Ship then sits at x=97 through the rest of wave 0's clock and all of wave 1 (columns
#   62/76/90 fire bullets at x=65/79/93, two pixels wide; the ship's box is [97,105), so
#   wave 1 cannot reach it however its re-fire jitter falls), and through wave 2's
#   90-tick intermission.
#
#   tick 670      wave 2 goes active; 8 enemies (4x2), none shot at, all still alive. Their
#                 first-volley fire schedule is 670 + 20 + 3*i for spawn index i = 0..7,
#                 i.e. 690, 693, ..., 711 — no RNG involved (see UpdateIntermission).
#   tick 711      the 8th and last enemy of the opening volley fires. At this exact tick all
#                 8 wave-2 enemies are alive and all 8 of their first-volley bullets are alive
#                 (the oldest, fired at 690, has fallen to y=45 of the 90 it needs to leave the
#                 screen) — the >=8-bullets/>=4-enemies load point the work order asks for.
#   ticks 712-730 hold Right: x 97 -> 116, out of every wave-2 column's bullet path (bullets
#                 sit at x=58/72/86/100 and are 2 px wide, so the rightmost hitbox ends at 102,
#                 well short of 116). The ship shares its column with column 3, whose bullets
#                 would have reached its row at ticks 752 and 754; the dodge is done at 730,
#                 22 ticks early, and x=116 is safe from every later volley too, jitter or not.
#   tick 870      wave 2's 200-tick clock runs out; it was the last wave, so the run ends in
#                 a win. The ship never lost a life the entire way — 3 lives spent, 0.
#
# Recorded/played with:
#   quarp replay record carts/shmup -o carts/shmup/replays/walkthrough.qrpr --ticks 900 \
#       --input-file carts/shmup/replays/walkthrough.input --every 20
1:L,22:O,23:R,37:O,38:R,52:O,53:R,67:O,68:,711:R,730:
